    "review::.::holistic::api_surface_coherence::executor_orchestrator_result_drift": {
      "id": "review::.::holistic::api_surface_coherence::executor_orchestrator_result_drift",
      "detector": "review",
      "file": ".",
      "tier": 1,
      "confidence": "high",
      "summary": "ExecutorRunResult and OrchestratorRunResult are deliberately paired but expose divergent serialization, error, and plan surfaces.",
      "detail": {
        "holistic": true,
        "dimension": "api_surface_coherence",
        "related_files": [
          "astrid/core/executor/runner.py",
          "astrid/core/orchestrator/runner.py"
        ],
        "evidence": [
          "OrchestratorRunResult (orchestrator/runner.py:98-131) has `to_dict()`, `errors: tuple[OrchestratorRunError, ...]`, `plan: OrchestratorPlan | None`, `outputs`, `runtime_kind`, `planned_commands`.",
          "ExecutorRunResult (executor/runner.py:71-87) has no `to_dict()`, no `errors`, no `plan`, instead carries `payload`, `skipped`, `skipped_reason`, `missing_binaries`.",
          "Both wrap the same conceptual run lifecycle and share `executor_id`/`kind`/`command`/`returncode`/`dry_run`/`ok`, so a developer naturally expects parity but finds different methods.",
          "Symmetric drift on the request side: OrchestratorRunRequest.out is `Path | str | None`; ExecutorRunRequest.out is `Path | str` (required), even though both are then re-validated at runtime."
        ],
        "suggestion": "Either factor a shared `RunResultBase` with `to_dict()` and converge on a common error/skip representation (e.g. a single `errors`/`skipped` field on both), or document explicitly why the two types diverge. At minimum, add `to_dict()` and a structured `errors` field to ExecutorRunResult so JSON-emitting CLIs don't have to special-case which result type they got.",
        "reasoning": "",
        "summary_hash": "c1bf436f"
