    "review::.::holistic::type_safety::normalize_effects_unchecked_cast": {
      "id": "review::.::holistic::type_safety::normalize_effects_unchecked_cast",
      "detector": "review",
      "file": ".",
      "tier": 2,
      "confidence": "medium",
      "summary": "`normalizeEffects(unknown): Effects` returns the input as-is via `as Effects` with no runtime shape check, so the typed return is a promise the function cannot keep",
      "detail": {
        "holistic": true,
        "dimension": "type_safety",
        "related_files": [
          "astrid/packs/local/elements/effects/_shared/clip-effects.ts"
        ],
        "evidence": [
          "Lines 16-22: `if (Array.isArray(effects)) { return effects.reduce<Effects>((acc, e) => ({...acc, ...(e as Effects)}), {}); } return effects as Effects;` \u2014 both branches assert `Effects` without checking that fade_in/fade_out/slide_in/slide_out are numbers (or absent). A clip with `effects: 'fade'` (string) or `effects: {fade_in: 'half'}` would propagate into `fadeOpacity`/`slideTransform` and only fail at `interpolate(...)` with a misleading runtime error.",
          "Downstream callers use `typeof effects.fade_in === 'number'` guards, so the runtime is forgiving \u2014 but the function's signature still oversells its safety."
        ],
        "suggestion": "Either (a) drop the cast and validate in place \u2014 pick out the four known numeric fields with `typeof v === 'number'` checks before assigning, returning a freshly-built `Effects` object \u2014 or (b) weaken the return type to `Partial<Effects>` and remove the `as Effects` cast on the array path so the spread builds the partial naturally. Option (a) is the small, honest change; the function is already 7 LOC.",
        "reasoning": "",
        "summary_hash": "7273d417"
      },
      "status": "fixed",
      "note": "not reported in latest holistic re-import",
