    "review::.::holistic::logic_clarity::codemod_merge_first_seen_isType": {
      "id": "review::.::holistic::logic_clarity::codemod_merge_first_seen_isType",
      "detector": "review",
      "file": ".",
      "tier": 2,
      "confidence": "medium",
      "summary": "mergeClauses locks isTypeOnly to the first occurrence of a named import",
      "detail": {
        "holistic": true,
        "dimension": "logic_clarity",
        "related_files": [
          "scripts/codemod-theme-api-imports.ts"
        ],
        "evidence": [
          "L168-171: `const key = ${n.name}::${n.alias ?? ''}; if (seen.has(key)) continue; \u2026 merged.named.push({...n, isTypeOnly: n.isTypeOnly || c.isTypeOnly});`",
          "If the same symbol appears in two clauses (one `import type { Foo }`, one `import { Foo }`), the first encountered wins, so a value import can be silently demoted to a type-only import (or vice versa) depending on source order."
        ],
        "suggestion": "After the loop, fold subsequent occurrences into the existing entry by `&&`-ing the per-name isTypeOnly across all clauses where it appears (a named import is type-only only if every occurrence is type-only). Alternatively, log when the same symbol appears with conflicting type-only states.",
